<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">SpecMamba: Accelerating Mamba Inference on FPGA with Speculative Decoding
<br class="ltx_break"><span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>
This work was supported in part by NSFC under Grant 62495102 and Grant 92464104, Grant 62341407, in part by the National Key Research and Development Program under Grant 2024YFB4505004, in part by Beijing Municipal Science and Technology Program under Grant Z241100004224015, and in part by 111 Project under Grant B18001.
<sup id="id13" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup>Equal contribution.
<sup id="id14" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">†</span></sup>Corresponding author.</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Linfeng Zhong<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1,3∗</span></sup>, Songqiang Xu<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1,4∗</span></sup>, Huifeng Wen<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1,4</span></sup>, Tong Xie<sup id="id4" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1,2</span></sup>, Qingyu Guo<sup id="id5" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup>, Yuan Wang<sup id="id6" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2,5</span></sup>, Meng Li<sup id="id7" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1,2,5†</span></sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><sup id="id8" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>Institute for Artificial Intelligence, Peking University, Beijing, China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><sup id="id9" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>School of Integrated Circuits, Peking University, Beijing, China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><sup id="id10" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>School of Electronic and Computer Engineering, Peking University, Shenzhen, China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><sup id="id11" class="ltx_sup">4</sup>School of Software and Microelectronics, Peking University, Beijing, China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><sup id="id12" class="ltx_sup">5</sup>Beijing Advanced Innovation Center for Integrated Circuits, Beijing, China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The growing demand for efficient long-sequence modeling on edge devices has propelled widespread adoption of State Space Models (SSMs) like Mamba, due to their superior computational efficiency and scalability. As its autoregressive generation process remains memory-bound, speculative decoding has been proposed that incorporates draft model generation and target model verification. However, directly applying speculative decoding to SSMs faces three key challenges:
(1) hidden state backtracking difficulties, (2) tree-based parallel verification incompatibility, and (3) hardware workload mismatch. To address these challenges, we propose SpecMamba, the first FPGA-based accelerator for Mamba with speculative decoding, which features system, algorithm, and hardware co-design. At the system level, we present a memory-aware hybrid backtracking strategy to coordinate both models. At the algorithm level, we propose first-in-first-out (FIFO)-based tree verification with tiling to minimize memory access. At the hardware level, we customize a dataflow that computes linear layers in parallel and SSM layers in series to enable maximal overlapping. Implemented on AMD FPGA platforms (VHK158 and VCK190), SpecMamba achieves a 2.27<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup over GPU baselines and a 2.85<math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> improvement compared to prior FPGA solutions, while demonstrating 5.41<math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> and 1.26<math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> higher energy efficiency, respectively.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Mamba, tree-based speculative decoding, FPGA accelerator, dataflow architecture

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">State Space Models (SSMs), like Mamba <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, have emerged as a competitive alternative to Transformer-based large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. With linear computational complexity, Mamba has exhibited superior demonstrated superior efficiency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> for long sequence modeling. Consequently, Mamba has undergone extensive development and refinement for various edge application scenarios, such as computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, multi-modal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, medical application <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, and point cloud processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite Mamba’s impressive efficiency gains,
its autoregressive generation process remains memory-bound,
leading to suboptimal computational utilization and thus limited throughput.
To mitigate this bandwidth constraint, speculative decoding has been proposed as a promising solution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>,
effectively increasing computational density and alleviating bandwidth constraints without compromising generation quality.
This technique employs a two-stage framework: (1) a smaller <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">draft model</span> generates potential token sequences autoregressively, while (2) a larger <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">target model</span> verifies these candidate tokens in parallel.
Candidate tokens can be further organized into tree structures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> to improve the acceptance rate of the draft tokens and the overall speedup ratio.
However, unlike Transformer-based models that leverage key-value cache (KV cache) to store and select previous tokens, Mamba models dynamically compress historical token information into a fixed-size hidden state,
impeding backtracking during speculative decoding, as depicted in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I introduction ‣ SpecMamba: Accelerating Mamba Inference on FPGA with Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Therefore, current research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> focuses primarily on (1) efficient verification of multiple candidate tokens within a single forward pass and (2) reliable state recovery to backtrack to the last valid hidden state after token rejection.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19873v1/intro_v2.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/218;" width="476" height="218"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Backtracking during speculative decoding. Transformers store all previous information in KV cache, while SSMs update and discard previous tokens, hindering backtracking.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19873v1/overall.svg" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/219;" width="476" height="219"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S1.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of <span id="S1.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SpecMamba</span>. (a) Dataflow of speculative
decoding. (b) Challenges of adapting speculative decoding for Mamba inference on FPGA. (c) Methods on <span id="S1.F2.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SpecMamba</span> from Sec. <a href="#S4" title="IV Memory-aware Hybrid Backtracking ‣ SpecMamba: Accelerating Mamba Inference on FPGA with Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> to Sec. <a href="#S6" title="VI linear parallel ssm sequential dataflow ‣ SpecMamba: Accelerating Mamba Inference on FPGA with Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>. (d) Results on DDR and HBM devices.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite promising, effectively accelerating Mamba with speculative decoding on edge devices, such as FPGAs, remains an open challenge.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> primarily target GPU platforms and would incur prohibitive overhead if directly deployed on FPGAs.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> have explored Mamba acceleration in edge devices using conventional techniques such as quantization and operator optimization. However, none of them supports speculative decoding.
Applying speculative decoding to FPGA-accelerated Mamba presents three fundamental challenges, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ I introduction ‣ SpecMamba: Accelerating Mamba Inference on FPGA with Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (b):
<span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Hidden states updating hinders state backtracking.</span>
Mamba discards previous hidden states after each update, complicating state recovery when draft tokens are rejected.
<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2): Tree-based parallel verification is not compatible with sequential processing.</span> SSMs’ sequential token processing precludes parallel path verification for tree-based drafts via attention masks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> as in Transformers.
<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Draft and target models workload mismatch leads to lower hardware utilization.</span>
The autoregressive draft model is memory-bound while the parallel verification of target model is compute-intensive. Making both work efficiently on the same hardware without wasting resources is crucial.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address the above challenges, we propose SpecMamba, the first FPGA-based accelerator that supports efficient Mamba inference with speculative decoding.
As shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ I introduction ‣ SpecMamba: Accelerating Mamba Inference on FPGA with Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (c),
At the system level, we present ❶
<span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory-aware hybrid backtracking</span>, which combines
off-chip state storage for draft models with on-chip activation
caching for target models.
At the algorithm level, we propose ❷
<span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">First-in-first-out (FIFO)-based tree verification with tiling</span> that enables tree-based speculative decoding in SSMs by exploiting inter-token dependencies for full-tree verification in a single pass.
At the hardware level, we employ
❸
<span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Linear Parallel SSM Sequential Dataflow</span>,
where we schedule linear layer computation in parallel and SSM computations in series to facilitate overlapping.
These optimizations achieve significant throughput improvements, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ I introduction ‣ SpecMamba: Accelerating Mamba Inference on FPGA with Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (d).
Our main contributions can be summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present the first framework that accelerates Mamba inference with speculative decoding on FPGAs. Our framework overcomes the incompatibilities between SSMs’ sequential processing and speculative decoding’s parallel verification requirements, enabling efficient high-speed inference on edge devices.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop a cross-level optimization approach that integrates: (i) a
memory-aware hybrid backtracking strategy, (ii) a novel FIFO-based tree verification with tiling algorithm, and (iii) a linear parallel SSM sequential dataflow.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our implementation on AMD platforms (VHK158 with HBM and VCK190 with DDR) achieves a 2.27× speedup and 5.41× higher energy efficiency compared to GPU baselines. Compared to prior FPGA-based works, SpecMamba demonstrates improvements of 2.85× in execution speed and 1.26× in energy efficiency.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">background and related work</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mamba</span></h3>

<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.19873v1/mamba_v2.svg" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:452/365;" width="452" height="365"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S2.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The model architecture of Mamba2 and the detailed computation graph of the SSM layer.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The architecture of Mamba is shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ II-A Mamba ‣ II background and related work ‣ SpecMamba: Accelerating Mamba Inference on FPGA with Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Each Mamba block consists of two linear projection layers (i.e., an input projection and an output projection), a 1-dimensional convolution layer, a residual connection, an SSM layer for sequence modeling, and two RMSNorm layers.
In each Mamba block, the input sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is processed to produce intermediate activations, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta,X,B,C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta,X,B,C</annotation></semantics></math>.
The SSM layer in Eq. <a href="#S2.E1" title="In II-A Mamba ‣ II background and related work ‣ SpecMamba: Accelerating Mamba Inference on FPGA with Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Eq. <a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Mamba ‣ II background and related work ‣ SpecMamba: Accelerating Mamba Inference on FPGA with Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> defines a linear mapping from an input signal <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to output signal <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> through a hidden state <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in each timestep <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bar{A}\otimes h_{t-1}+\bar{B}\otimes X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bar{A}\otimes h_{t-1}+\bar{B}\otimes X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=C\times h_{t-1}+D\otimes X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=C\times h_{t-1}+D\otimes X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\otimes" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\otimes</annotation></semantics></math> denotes element-wise multiplication.
A typical configuration (e.g., Mamba-2-2.7B with <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=80</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=64," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=64,</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=128</annotation></semantics></math>) reveals that the hidden state <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times p\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times p\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> may incur over 1 GB memory overhead during parallel verification,
thousands of times greater than activations such as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">During SSM processing, the input <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> undergoes a sequence of transformations. Specifically, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is involved in element-wise multiplications with parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> separately, generating <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is then multiplied with the hidden state <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is element-wise multiplied with input <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The outcomes of these operations are then added to obtain an updated hidden state <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is multiplied by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by a linear transformation. The result is then added to the element-wise product of the input <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and parameter <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, generating the final output <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Speculative decoding</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Speculative decoding leverages smaller language models, referred to as draft models, to generate draft tokens, and adopts the original larger model, named target models, to verify draft tokens in parallel.
When a token fails verification,
the next iteration of draft generation and parallel verification rolls back from the last accepted draft tokens.
To improve the acceptance rate, recent approaches adopt tree-based verification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> rather than sequence-based verification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, which construct and verify a tree composed of draft tokens, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ I introduction ‣ SpecMamba: Accelerating Mamba Inference on FPGA with Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (a). By simultaneously considering more diverse speculation candidates (instead of just one as in sequence-based approaches), tree-based approaches can significantly boost the number of tokens generated in a single forward pass.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As depicted in Fig. <a href="#S1.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ I introduction ‣ SpecMamba: Accelerating Mamba Inference on FPGA with Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (a), while Transformer-based models use attention masks to restrict tokens
(e.g., <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“banana”</span>)) to attend only their parent nodes (e.g., <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“Tom”</span>) and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“eats”</span>)) in the tree structure, Mamba models lack an analogous mechanism for selective token attention.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address this, Snakes and Ladders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> introduced two approaches, the first recovers states by recomputing from augmented input sequences, and the second avoids intensive linear computation by maintaining intermediate activations. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> preserved hidden states for both models, restoring them through selective recomputation during inference.
However, these methods are designed for GPUs and require extensive tensor transfer, leading to unacceptable communication and memory overhead in bandwidth-limited FPGA platforms.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S2.T1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE I</span>: </span><span id="S2.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison between different LLM Accelerators.</span></figcaption>
