<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We compare SpecMamba with the prior-art FPGA accelerator, LightMamba<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, across two hardware settings: HBM-FPGA and DDR-FPGA in Table <a href="#S7.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ VII-B Evaluation Results ‣ VII experiments ‣ SpecMamba: Accelerating Mamba Inference on FPGA with Speculative Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>.
Both works employ Mamba2-2.7B as the target model, while SpecMamba integrates Mamba2-370M as the draft model. For the HBM scenario, SpecMamba achieves a practical throughput of 313.4 tokens/s on the VHK158 FPGA, which outperforms LightMamba on the U280 FPGA by 3.37<math id="S7.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> on average.
For the DDR scenario, SpecMamba achieves a throughput of 20.6 tokens/s
on the VCK190 FPGA, which is 2.85<math id="S7.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>
higher than
LightMamba.
SpecMamba consistently outperforms prior works in both high-bandwidth and bandwidth-limited conditions.</p>
</div>
